  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: b1b1198fa5b13d047b3edb9610bc8681, type: 3}
  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: Assembly-CSharp::InteractableTarget
  targetTitle: Challenger Deep
  subTitle: 
  descriptionParagraphs:
  - "Fast FactsDepth: Approximately 10,935 meters (35,876 feet), which is nearly
    11 kilometers or about 7 miles deep. If you placed Mount Everest inside, its
    peak would still be covered by over 2 kilometers of water.Pressure: The hydrostatic
    pressure at the bottom is over 1,000 times the standard atmospheric pressure
    at sea level. This is powerful enough to crush most standard submarines.Environment:
    The water temperature fluctuates just above freezing, between 1\xB0C and 4\xB0C.
    It is also a zone of permanent, absolute darkness.Historical Explorations1875:
    It was first discovered and recorded by the British survey ship HMS Challenger,
    which gave the deep its name.1960: The first human descent was made by Jacques
    Piccard and Don Walsh inside the oceanographic bathyscaphe Trieste.2012: Film
    director James Cameron made the first solo dive to the bottom in the submersible
    Deepsea Challenger.2019: Victor Vescovo completed multiple manned descents with
    the submersible Limiting Factor, breaking records for the deepest ocean dives."
  displayImages:
  - {fileID: 21300000, guid: 3f28c2128220c8a4896b6e662006afc5, type: 3}
